WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.113 --> 00:00:06.280
VINAY BALASUBRAMANIAM: Good
morning, good afternoon,

00:00:06.280 --> 00:00:08.280
good evening, and thanks
for joining the session

00:00:08.280 --> 00:00:10.210
on Inside BigQuery BI Engine.

00:00:10.210 --> 00:00:11.490
I'm Vinay Balasubramaniam.

00:00:11.490 --> 00:00:13.650
I'm a product
manager on BigQuery.

00:00:13.650 --> 00:00:15.380
I'm joined by Mosha
Pasumansky, who

00:00:15.380 --> 00:00:17.910
is a principal
engineer on BigQuery.

00:00:17.910 --> 00:00:19.920
So in today's session,
we will talk about,

00:00:19.920 --> 00:00:22.010
give an overview
of what BigQuery BI

00:00:22.010 --> 00:00:25.970
Engine is, why we built it, what
is the core value proposition.

00:00:25.970 --> 00:00:28.910
We talk about, next we go into
the internals of BI engine,

00:00:28.910 --> 00:00:31.790
and we spend a the rest
of most of the talk

00:00:31.790 --> 00:00:35.010
in talking about
how BI Engine works.

00:00:35.010 --> 00:00:37.230
In the end we'll have some
exciting demos to showcase

00:00:37.230 --> 00:00:40.570
how BI Engine handles
higher concurrency.

00:00:40.570 --> 00:00:43.050
And then we'll have
Madeleine from Tableau

00:00:43.050 --> 00:00:45.990
showcasing a demo of
BI Engine with Tableau,

00:00:45.990 --> 00:00:48.030
and we'll have Kevin
from Looker talk

00:00:48.030 --> 00:00:50.070
about how BI Engine
works with Looker.

00:00:50.070 --> 00:00:52.270
So let's get started.

00:00:52.270 --> 00:00:56.100
Doing BI on big data is quite
challenging for customers, too.

00:00:56.100 --> 00:00:59.620
So customers today have to
manage multiple data marts

00:00:59.620 --> 00:01:01.940
around the data
warehouse, extract data,

00:01:01.940 --> 00:01:06.530
manage complex ETL pipeline
to make the BI tools.

00:01:06.530 --> 00:01:08.670
And that creates additional
management overhead

00:01:08.670 --> 00:01:11.230
and administration [? hold. ?]

00:01:11.230 --> 00:01:13.980
Secondly, today
traditional BI applications

00:01:13.980 --> 00:01:16.410
are used to extracting data
from the data warehouse,

00:01:16.410 --> 00:01:20.580
creating OLAP cubes, running
[? IT ?] jobs to refresh those

00:01:20.580 --> 00:01:21.540
cubes.

00:01:21.540 --> 00:01:24.090
So they end up looking
at the dashboard, which

00:01:24.090 --> 00:01:25.782
is on all data.

00:01:25.782 --> 00:01:27.240
More and more
customers are looking

00:01:27.240 --> 00:01:29.430
for not only fast and
interactive dashboard,

00:01:29.430 --> 00:01:31.770
but also they're looking
at more real time data

00:01:31.770 --> 00:01:34.200
so that they can get insights
faster and take action

00:01:34.200 --> 00:01:35.710
accordingly.

00:01:35.710 --> 00:01:38.410
And lastly organization,
as they scale globally,

00:01:38.410 --> 00:01:41.090
they want to share their
information and insights

00:01:41.090 --> 00:01:42.840
not only to internal
stakeholders,

00:01:42.840 --> 00:01:45.870
but also to external
partners and customers.

00:01:45.870 --> 00:01:49.640
So this adds a lot of additional
concurrency requirements

00:01:49.640 --> 00:01:51.950
for their BI solutions.

00:01:51.950 --> 00:01:53.540
These are some of
the reasons why we

00:01:53.540 --> 00:01:55.850
wanted to build the BI Engine.

00:01:55.850 --> 00:01:59.060
If we look at GCP Smart
Analytics platform,

00:01:59.060 --> 00:02:02.470
we provide solutions across
all the different life cycles

00:02:02.470 --> 00:02:06.040
of BI, starting from connecting
to multiple data sources,

00:02:06.040 --> 00:02:08.350
whether it's on prem
sources or software

00:02:08.350 --> 00:02:09.910
as a service end points.

00:02:09.910 --> 00:02:13.440
We can bring in data from
multiple sources into BigQuery.

00:02:13.440 --> 00:02:15.970
And as part of
that transformation

00:02:15.970 --> 00:02:18.910
we provide tools such
as Cloud Dataflow

00:02:18.910 --> 00:02:21.640
to do transformation and
enrichment on your streaming

00:02:21.640 --> 00:02:22.360
data.

00:02:22.360 --> 00:02:25.750
You have Cloud Data Fusion,
which looks at traditional ETL

00:02:25.750 --> 00:02:27.490
style transformation.

00:02:27.490 --> 00:02:31.120
Then we have Dataproc for Spark
and Hadoop sort of workflow.

00:02:31.120 --> 00:02:35.710
Once the data is in BigQuery,
you can create a metadata layer

00:02:35.710 --> 00:02:39.010
to discover data
through data catalog,

00:02:39.010 --> 00:02:42.140
you can use Cloud Dataprep
for data wrangling,

00:02:42.140 --> 00:02:44.690
and then you can connect
the data from BigQuery

00:02:44.690 --> 00:02:46.380
to multiple BI tools.

00:02:46.380 --> 00:02:50.240
We provide first party BI
solutions, Looker, Data Studio,

00:02:50.240 --> 00:02:51.200
and Sheets.

00:02:51.200 --> 00:02:54.980
We also have connectors from our
rich ecosystem of BI partners,

00:02:54.980 --> 00:02:58.440
including Tableau Power
BI, MicroStrategy and Click

00:02:58.440 --> 00:02:59.950
[INAUDIBLE].

00:02:59.950 --> 00:03:01.600
But where BI Engine
is really helpful

00:03:01.600 --> 00:03:05.250
is it acts as an acceleration
layer between BigQuery

00:03:05.250 --> 00:03:06.720
and your BI tool.

00:03:06.720 --> 00:03:11.430
So all, not just Data Studio,
other BI tools can use this.

00:03:11.430 --> 00:03:13.500
So the overall vision
for BigQuery BI Engine

00:03:13.500 --> 00:03:17.250
is to democratize BI,
enabling business analysts

00:03:17.250 --> 00:03:22.110
to perform interactive
analytics in real time.

00:03:22.110 --> 00:03:24.930
So today BI tools compromise
between data freshness

00:03:24.930 --> 00:03:25.900
and speed.

00:03:25.900 --> 00:03:28.780
So if you want data to be
really fast and interactive,

00:03:28.780 --> 00:03:29.880
it's not fresh.

00:03:29.880 --> 00:03:31.980
But if you want data to
be fresh and latest data

00:03:31.980 --> 00:03:35.570
without any staleness,
it's not fast.

00:03:35.570 --> 00:03:37.020
With BigQuery BI
engine, we don't

00:03:37.020 --> 00:03:38.978
want to compromise between
freshness and speed.

00:03:38.978 --> 00:03:40.890
We always want to
give you fresh data

00:03:40.890 --> 00:03:43.300
and also it always
has to be fast.

00:03:43.300 --> 00:03:46.140
Now, three key value
propositions of BigQuery BI

00:03:46.140 --> 00:03:46.820
Engine.

00:03:46.820 --> 00:03:50.830
It provides sub-second queries,
it simplifies the architecture,

00:03:50.830 --> 00:03:54.773
and it has smart tuning
built. Let's go into each one.

00:03:54.773 --> 00:03:56.440
To achieve sub-second
queries, BI Engine

00:03:56.440 --> 00:04:00.320
is a column-oriented
in-memory execution engine.

00:04:00.320 --> 00:04:03.530
It can scale horizontally so as
you have more requests coming

00:04:03.530 --> 00:04:06.530
in, the engine can load
balance that request

00:04:06.530 --> 00:04:09.320
by making copies of
data in memory and load

00:04:09.320 --> 00:04:10.820
balance those requests.

00:04:10.820 --> 00:04:13.190
And lastly BI Engine works
with BigQuery streaming.

00:04:13.190 --> 00:04:16.040
So as you're streaming data
in, the data is automatically

00:04:16.040 --> 00:04:17.760
refreshed into the
in-memory queue

00:04:17.760 --> 00:04:20.250
so you get the latest data.

00:04:20.250 --> 00:04:22.890
In terms of simplification
of architecture,

00:04:22.890 --> 00:04:26.360
BI engine is built on
top of BigQuery storage.

00:04:26.360 --> 00:04:29.120
So there's no need to
extract data out of BigQuery.

00:04:29.120 --> 00:04:31.490
We automatically move
data between the storage

00:04:31.490 --> 00:04:33.830
tier and the memory tier.

00:04:33.830 --> 00:04:36.200
And traditional
BI tools required

00:04:36.200 --> 00:04:40.970
you to create OLAP cubes, but we
do that automatically for you.

00:04:40.970 --> 00:04:43.490
That reduces complexity
in architecture.

00:04:43.490 --> 00:04:46.610
And lastly, today, BI engine
is available to Data Studio,

00:04:46.610 --> 00:04:48.440
but what we're
bringing to market

00:04:48.440 --> 00:04:53.400
is BI Engine SQL API, which is
integrated with BigQuery API

00:04:53.400 --> 00:04:54.360
directly.

00:04:54.360 --> 00:04:57.920
So any of the other BI tools,
including Looker, Tableau,

00:04:57.920 --> 00:05:01.220
Power BI, Click,
MicroStrategy, et cetera,

00:05:01.220 --> 00:05:03.920
can leverage that
without having to make

00:05:03.920 --> 00:05:07.130
any change on their side.

00:05:07.130 --> 00:05:10.400
And lastly, there are smart
tuning built into BI Engine.

00:05:10.400 --> 00:05:14.360
So BI Engine has inbuilt
cache optimizations,

00:05:14.360 --> 00:05:17.310
which we do automatically
based on your data.

00:05:17.310 --> 00:05:19.867
And the way you do that is
you create a reservation

00:05:19.867 --> 00:05:22.450
where you specify the location
you want the reservation to be,

00:05:22.450 --> 00:05:24.930
specify the size, and
automatically BI Engine

00:05:24.930 --> 00:05:28.800
takes care of tuning the data
based on your query patterns.

00:05:28.800 --> 00:05:32.940
And all of this data, which
is available to Stackdriver,

00:05:32.940 --> 00:05:35.422
so you can look at how
much cache is being used,

00:05:35.422 --> 00:05:37.380
how much memory is being
used, how much of that

00:05:37.380 --> 00:05:40.260
is coming from cache, how much
it's falling back to BigQuery.

00:05:40.260 --> 00:05:42.850
All of this rich
information and metrics

00:05:42.850 --> 00:05:45.135
is available at BI engine.

00:05:45.135 --> 00:05:47.010
Well, that was a quick
overview of BI engine.

00:05:47.010 --> 00:05:50.460
Now let's go into how BI
Engine works in the internals.

00:05:50.460 --> 00:05:54.010
Let me introduce Mosha to
you to walk us through that.

00:05:54.010 --> 00:05:56.130
MOSHA PASUMANSKY:
Thank you Vinay.

00:05:56.130 --> 00:05:58.190
My name is Mosha
Pasumansky, and I'm

00:05:58.190 --> 00:06:02.060
very excited to talk today with
you about the inner workings

00:06:02.060 --> 00:06:04.380
of BigQuery BI Engine.

00:06:04.380 --> 00:06:07.430
So we start with the high
level architecture of BigQuery.

00:06:07.430 --> 00:06:10.250
And we see three
main pieces here.

00:06:10.250 --> 00:06:12.110
On the left is storage.

00:06:12.110 --> 00:06:15.350
That's where BigQuery
stores all of its data.

00:06:15.350 --> 00:06:16.940
On the right, compute.

00:06:16.940 --> 00:06:19.880
That's where query
processing is happening.

00:06:19.880 --> 00:06:22.940
And between them shuffle
layer, which facilitates

00:06:22.940 --> 00:06:25.540
distributed processing.

00:06:25.540 --> 00:06:30.060
Now let's see where BI Engine
fits into this picture.

00:06:30.060 --> 00:06:33.370
We see BI Engine is just
another feature of BigQuery.

00:06:33.370 --> 00:06:35.170
It's not a separate product.

00:06:35.170 --> 00:06:38.290
So it works on top of the
same storage, same network,

00:06:38.290 --> 00:06:40.920
same shuffle layer,
and it [? worker ?]

00:06:40.920 --> 00:06:44.290
run alongside of
BigQuery workers.

00:06:44.290 --> 00:06:49.120
OK, well if it's exactly
same architecture, what

00:06:49.120 --> 00:06:51.850
makes it possible
for BI Engine to get

00:06:51.850 --> 00:06:54.500
such dramatic
performance improvements?

00:06:54.500 --> 00:06:57.680
And this is exactly what
we're going to talk about now.

00:06:57.680 --> 00:07:00.100
So we see there are
three big pieces.

00:07:00.100 --> 00:07:03.850
And all three of them
contribute to the performance

00:07:03.850 --> 00:07:04.810
of the query.

00:07:04.810 --> 00:07:09.760
Storage is a disk I/O time,
shuffle is a network I/O time,

00:07:09.760 --> 00:07:11.780
and compute is CPU.

00:07:11.780 --> 00:07:13.300
So what we're going
to do now, we'll

00:07:13.300 --> 00:07:15.970
pick each one of
those three components

00:07:15.970 --> 00:07:20.500
and talk how BI Engine
makes this time go to zero.

00:07:20.500 --> 00:07:23.060
OK, let's start
with the disk I/O.

00:07:23.060 --> 00:07:25.510
And this one may seem
to be the most obvious,

00:07:25.510 --> 00:07:27.220
because we just
put data in memory

00:07:27.220 --> 00:07:29.920
and there is no disk
I/O. And of course, it

00:07:29.920 --> 00:07:32.635
would have been that simple
if we had unlimited memory

00:07:32.635 --> 00:07:35.380
and we can put all
the data into it.

00:07:35.380 --> 00:07:37.810
But of course it's
more complex than that.

00:07:37.810 --> 00:07:40.750
We'll have to manage and figure
out what to keep in memory

00:07:40.750 --> 00:07:42.370
and what to leave on disk.

00:07:42.370 --> 00:07:44.470
And that's where
the BI Engine memory

00:07:44.470 --> 00:07:46.240
manager comes into the picture.

00:07:46.240 --> 00:07:48.650
This is really the
most important piece.

00:07:48.650 --> 00:07:51.190
It's the heart of the BI Engine.

00:07:51.190 --> 00:07:52.780
So let's see how it works.

00:07:52.780 --> 00:07:56.230
First of all, BigQuery is
a column-based database,

00:07:56.230 --> 00:07:59.890
so we only put specific
columns into memory, not

00:07:59.890 --> 00:08:01.240
the whole table.

00:08:01.240 --> 00:08:04.650
But more than that, we're not
putting the entire column;

00:08:04.650 --> 00:08:06.640
we're only putting chunks of it.

00:08:06.640 --> 00:08:10.480
And that works equally well
with both partition tables

00:08:10.480 --> 00:08:12.350
and not partition tables.

00:08:12.350 --> 00:08:14.910
So even if your table is
not partitioned from time

00:08:14.910 --> 00:08:17.560
and it keeps data going
multiple years back,

00:08:17.560 --> 00:08:20.110
but you're only
querying last few days,

00:08:20.110 --> 00:08:22.570
memory manager
will figure it out.

00:08:22.570 --> 00:08:26.630
Next, memory manager not
only keeps the raw data,

00:08:26.630 --> 00:08:28.690
it also keeps
direct calculations,

00:08:28.690 --> 00:08:33.650
like intermediate results
of aggregations and so on.

00:08:33.650 --> 00:08:37.820
Also we talked about
how BI Engine is always

00:08:37.820 --> 00:08:39.230
fast and always fresh.

00:08:39.230 --> 00:08:43.230
So to be always fresh and
not to serve stale data,

00:08:43.230 --> 00:08:46.880
it has very tight integration
with metadata service.

00:08:46.880 --> 00:08:49.610
It knows exactly when
transactions commit.

00:08:49.610 --> 00:08:53.390
It keeps exactly the
same snapshot timestamps

00:08:53.390 --> 00:08:56.990
as the rest of BigQuery, so
whenever new data comes in,

00:08:56.990 --> 00:08:58.940
it knows how to
pick it up and mesh

00:08:58.940 --> 00:09:00.830
with existing data in memory.

00:09:00.830 --> 00:09:04.410
Even when data changes
through [INAUDIBLE] memory

00:09:04.410 --> 00:09:06.740
manager knows exactly
which parts of it

00:09:06.740 --> 00:09:10.280
should be invalidated
and which should not.

00:09:10.280 --> 00:09:14.840
Also memory manager knows how
to work with load balancing.

00:09:14.840 --> 00:09:17.600
Even if you have small
table, but with a lot

00:09:17.600 --> 00:09:20.270
of querying coming
in, it may choose

00:09:20.270 --> 00:09:23.750
to implicate same data over
multiple machines, which

00:09:23.750 --> 00:09:25.680
is easy for us to
do because BigQuery

00:09:25.680 --> 00:09:27.810
is distributed database.

00:09:27.810 --> 00:09:32.090
So this way it will use small
memory, but all of the queries

00:09:32.090 --> 00:09:34.850
will still have very
fast response time

00:09:34.850 --> 00:09:38.180
because the load will be spread.

00:09:38.180 --> 00:09:41.340
So you can see, it's a
pretty sophisticated system.

00:09:41.340 --> 00:09:45.200
Now let's see what kind of
control users have over it.

00:09:45.200 --> 00:09:49.330
And this control comes in
form of BI reservations.

00:09:49.330 --> 00:09:53.180
BI reservations are very
similar to classic BigQuery

00:09:53.180 --> 00:09:56.390
reservations, but the
currency is memory.

00:09:56.390 --> 00:10:00.800
So you are buying memory
and not slots or CPU.

00:10:00.800 --> 00:10:03.370
All the metrics we serve
through Stackdriver,

00:10:03.370 --> 00:10:07.160
so the users can understand
how efficiently they're

00:10:07.160 --> 00:10:08.880
using the system.

00:10:08.880 --> 00:10:12.140
For example, in this
picture you see two lines.

00:10:12.140 --> 00:10:16.250
Blue one is how much
reservation user bought,

00:10:16.250 --> 00:10:19.900
and the orange one is
how much there was used.

00:10:19.900 --> 00:10:22.070
So user can save,
they bought too much

00:10:22.070 --> 00:10:24.050
and there is too
much unused, they

00:10:24.050 --> 00:10:27.500
can lower the reservation
size and it takes effect

00:10:27.500 --> 00:10:29.490
immediately.

00:10:29.490 --> 00:10:33.300
And when they see that the
orange start bumping into blue,

00:10:33.300 --> 00:10:35.690
meaning that the
reservation is not

00:10:35.690 --> 00:10:37.750
enough for the
current workload, user

00:10:37.750 --> 00:10:40.470
may choose to buy a little
bit more reservation

00:10:40.470 --> 00:10:43.910
and balance it like that.

00:10:43.910 --> 00:10:48.830
OK, so we covered how we
deal with storage component.

00:10:48.830 --> 00:10:52.100
Now let's talk about
compute or CPU.

00:10:52.100 --> 00:10:56.940
There are many, many techniques
that we use to improve CPU.

00:10:56.940 --> 00:10:58.970
I'll talk about
a couple of them.

00:10:58.970 --> 00:11:01.880
First one is
vectorized processing.

00:11:01.880 --> 00:11:07.430
So what you see on the left is
the classic way how databases

00:11:07.430 --> 00:11:11.450
deal, their query engine dealt
with query processing, which

00:11:11.450 --> 00:11:14.090
is called sometimes
[INAUDIBLE] iterator or tuple

00:11:14.090 --> 00:11:15.350
iterator model.

00:11:15.350 --> 00:11:18.170
Basically it says,
query plan comes

00:11:18.170 --> 00:11:21.830
as a [? trio ?] of iterators,
and those iterators

00:11:21.830 --> 00:11:23.620
consume one row at a time.

00:11:23.620 --> 00:11:25.910
Process it, send to
the next iterator,

00:11:25.910 --> 00:11:28.230
takes next row, and so on.

00:11:28.230 --> 00:11:32.690
So this is very general and
very well understood model,

00:11:32.690 --> 00:11:35.620
but it's not the best
one, especially when we're

00:11:35.620 --> 00:11:38.330
dealing with column-based
data structure

00:11:38.330 --> 00:11:40.670
and within memory databases.

00:11:40.670 --> 00:11:44.630
So in recent year another model
called vectorized processing

00:11:44.630 --> 00:11:46.100
became more popular.

00:11:46.100 --> 00:11:48.730
Here instead of
adding the whole row

00:11:48.730 --> 00:11:51.270
and sending it to the
[? trio ?] of operators,

00:11:51.270 --> 00:11:56.550
we pick single column at a
time, read like 1,000 values,

00:11:56.550 --> 00:11:59.430
process them in tight loop,
and then send forward.

00:11:59.430 --> 00:12:02.300
So this is much
more complex model,

00:12:02.300 --> 00:12:03.800
but you don't need
to worry about it

00:12:03.800 --> 00:12:05.310
because we took care of it.

00:12:05.310 --> 00:12:07.970
We wrote the entire
runtime in BigQuery

00:12:07.970 --> 00:12:12.020
to use vectorized separators,
and we put this new runtime

00:12:12.020 --> 00:12:14.390
into BI Engine.

00:12:14.390 --> 00:12:17.440
So there are a lot of
benefits for CPU for it.

00:12:17.440 --> 00:12:19.790
First, the cache
locality is much better

00:12:19.790 --> 00:12:23.600
because you run very,
very localized loops.

00:12:23.600 --> 00:12:27.300
And second, your code
doesn't jump around between

00:12:27.300 --> 00:12:29.450
[? operator ?] to
[? operator, ?] so we can fill

00:12:29.450 --> 00:12:32.750
the CPU pipeline much tighter.

00:12:32.750 --> 00:12:36.440
We also take advantage
of modern CPU instruction

00:12:36.440 --> 00:12:39.260
called SMG
instructions which can

00:12:39.260 --> 00:12:44.330
process many values at the
same clock cycle and so on.

00:12:44.330 --> 00:12:46.990
So we've got great
benefits from it.

00:12:46.990 --> 00:12:49.100
Another technique
that we're using

00:12:49.100 --> 00:12:53.520
is we're using many
different encodings.

00:12:53.520 --> 00:12:58.890
So we already have support for
a few encodings in our storage

00:12:58.890 --> 00:12:59.730
system.

00:12:59.730 --> 00:13:02.620
But now that the
data is in memory,

00:13:02.620 --> 00:13:05.510
we have the liberty of
using many, many more,

00:13:05.510 --> 00:13:07.910
because it's much
easier to change them

00:13:07.910 --> 00:13:09.670
in a memory representation.

00:13:09.670 --> 00:13:12.000
I will touch couple
of them here.

00:13:12.000 --> 00:13:16.850
First one is the most well-known
one, is a dictionary encoding.

00:13:16.850 --> 00:13:20.030
It works really well then you
have a table with millions

00:13:20.030 --> 00:13:24.320
of rows but certain column has
only a few distinct values,

00:13:24.320 --> 00:13:28.070
like maybe 100 or in
this picture only three.

00:13:28.070 --> 00:13:30.380
And the column is
the name of the song.

00:13:30.380 --> 00:13:32.390
So when you have
complex expression,

00:13:32.390 --> 00:13:35.300
instead of computing
it again and again

00:13:35.300 --> 00:13:37.710
over each one of
those million values,

00:13:37.710 --> 00:13:40.220
you just compute it
over the dictionary

00:13:40.220 --> 00:13:44.180
and keep the result. In this
case, the result is [INAUDIBLE]

00:13:44.180 --> 00:13:46.370
but it can [INAUDIBLE].

00:13:46.370 --> 00:13:48.740
Now this is, by the
way, an example of one

00:13:48.740 --> 00:13:51.490
of the derived calculations
that I talked earlier when

00:13:51.490 --> 00:13:53.630
we talked about memory manager.

00:13:53.630 --> 00:13:55.310
So the benefit is huge.

00:13:55.310 --> 00:13:57.740
Instead of doing computation
millions of times,

00:13:57.740 --> 00:14:00.230
we'll do it only three times.

00:14:00.230 --> 00:14:03.880
Dictionary encoding can be
used in many other operators,

00:14:03.880 --> 00:14:08.840
in aggregations, in joins,
in analytic functions.

00:14:08.840 --> 00:14:10.390
So it's a great technique.

00:14:10.390 --> 00:14:12.980
Another technique is
run length encoding.

00:14:12.980 --> 00:14:15.340
So you can see example
on the right here.

00:14:15.340 --> 00:14:17.440
It works really
well when you have

00:14:17.440 --> 00:14:20.750
same value in the column
repeated many times in a row.

00:14:20.750 --> 00:14:23.170
So here we have
example with quarters.

00:14:23.170 --> 00:14:25.600
And we have quarter
one three times

00:14:25.600 --> 00:14:27.530
and quarter two four times.

00:14:27.530 --> 00:14:30.610
So again, instead of
computing the same expression

00:14:30.610 --> 00:14:34.330
three times, we'll compute
it once and just say oh,

00:14:34.330 --> 00:14:37.830
and please give this
result three times.

00:14:37.830 --> 00:14:40.590
Now we'll have dozens
of different encodings,

00:14:40.590 --> 00:14:42.990
and they can stack one
on top of the other

00:14:42.990 --> 00:14:44.850
like you can use both
dictionary [INAUDIBLE]

00:14:44.850 --> 00:14:48.700
for example and many others.

00:14:48.700 --> 00:14:52.410
So again, BI Engine takes
care of it all automatically.

00:14:52.410 --> 00:14:56.220
It looks at the data, its
statistics and query history,

00:14:56.220 --> 00:15:00.410
and figures out which encoding
is going to be the best.

00:15:00.410 --> 00:15:04.030
So this is how we deal
with CPU, now let's go

00:15:04.030 --> 00:15:06.290
and talk how we
deal with network.

00:15:06.290 --> 00:15:10.120
How can we eliminate
network or reduce it?

00:15:10.120 --> 00:15:13.390
So the way we approach it
is from the query planner

00:15:13.390 --> 00:15:14.710
perspective.

00:15:14.710 --> 00:15:19.360
Basically we look at the query
plans that BigQuery generates

00:15:19.360 --> 00:15:22.640
and most of those plans
are distributed plans.

00:15:22.640 --> 00:15:25.060
And then we say, OK
how we can improve it,

00:15:25.060 --> 00:15:26.980
given that we have
this BI Engine

00:15:26.980 --> 00:15:30.290
with its stateful workers
and different scheduling

00:15:30.290 --> 00:15:33.290
policy and the memory data.

00:15:33.290 --> 00:15:35.940
So let's again pick
a couple of examples.

00:15:35.940 --> 00:15:39.730
What you can see here is a
very simple aggregation query.

00:15:39.730 --> 00:15:42.490
And the distributed plan
in the classic BigQuery

00:15:42.490 --> 00:15:44.110
will be two stages.

00:15:44.110 --> 00:15:46.180
First stage will
do some filtering

00:15:46.180 --> 00:15:49.270
and then do
preliminary aggregation

00:15:49.270 --> 00:15:51.520
and will compute
intermediate results.

00:15:51.520 --> 00:15:54.100
Then we'll send them
all over the network

00:15:54.100 --> 00:15:57.150
to compute the final result. OK?

00:15:57.150 --> 00:16:00.880
With BI Engine, here's
the plan with BI Engine.

00:16:00.880 --> 00:16:04.430
So it's funny because it
looks almost exactly the same,

00:16:04.430 --> 00:16:07.090
but now instead of
doing distributed plan,

00:16:07.090 --> 00:16:08.860
we're running parallel plan.

00:16:08.860 --> 00:16:12.900
Basically our scheduler knows
where each piece of data

00:16:12.900 --> 00:16:16.180
resides on each worker,
can bundle them together

00:16:16.180 --> 00:16:20.140
and say, run the same plan but
this time not over the network.

00:16:20.140 --> 00:16:22.300
Run it as a parallel plan, OK?

00:16:22.300 --> 00:16:26.770
Do this local aggregation
locally, and then also

00:16:26.770 --> 00:16:30.340
measure the result also locally.

00:16:30.340 --> 00:16:32.680
And now only after
that you can send it

00:16:32.680 --> 00:16:34.120
to the distributed execution.

00:16:34.120 --> 00:16:37.630
So network traffic
is reduced greatly.

00:16:37.630 --> 00:16:40.850
Let's look into another little
bit more complex example.

00:16:40.850 --> 00:16:43.180
So here, we're
talking about joins.

00:16:43.180 --> 00:16:46.000
And there are many
join plans in BigQuery

00:16:46.000 --> 00:16:49.390
but one pretty popular one
is called Broadcast Join.

00:16:49.390 --> 00:16:52.270
It works very well when
you have a big table,

00:16:52.270 --> 00:16:55.370
let's say a fact table,
joining with a smaller

00:16:55.370 --> 00:16:58.050
one like dimension table.

00:16:58.050 --> 00:16:59.870
So a typical plan
in BigQuery would

00:16:59.870 --> 00:17:04.130
be to take this small table
and broadcast into all workers,

00:17:04.130 --> 00:17:06.720
and broadcast goes
over the network.

00:17:06.720 --> 00:17:08.640
Now what we can
do with BI Engine,

00:17:08.640 --> 00:17:11.000
our memory manager can
look into it and say,

00:17:11.000 --> 00:17:14.710
you know what, this small
table is small enough.

00:17:14.710 --> 00:17:17.810
Why don't I just
replicate it across all

00:17:17.810 --> 00:17:21.960
of the nodes which have the
pieces of the fact table.

00:17:21.960 --> 00:17:23.720
So it will use a
little bit more memory.

00:17:23.720 --> 00:17:27.530
But now, join can happen
completely locally.

00:17:27.530 --> 00:17:30.030
We don't need to
use network at all.

00:17:30.030 --> 00:17:32.660
OK, so this kind of
decision memory manager

00:17:32.660 --> 00:17:35.330
can do because it knows
the reservation size,

00:17:35.330 --> 00:17:38.150
it knows the query
workload, so it can do

00:17:38.150 --> 00:17:39.720
this kind of automatic tuning.

00:17:39.720 --> 00:17:43.970
OK so now we covered all three
big components of BigQuery

00:17:43.970 --> 00:17:46.210
architecture and
talked a little bit how

00:17:46.210 --> 00:17:47.970
we improve each one of them.

00:17:47.970 --> 00:17:51.990
And it's time to see
how it actually works.

00:17:51.990 --> 00:17:54.080
We're going to show
you three demos.

00:17:54.080 --> 00:17:56.540
We're starting
with the first one

00:17:56.540 --> 00:18:01.040
to demonstrate how BI Engine
handles high concurrency.

00:18:01.040 --> 00:18:05.510
And this is artificial
demo which we simulated,

00:18:05.510 --> 00:18:07.310
and it simulates the
following situation.

00:18:07.310 --> 00:18:09.450
Let's say you have
a company and you

00:18:09.450 --> 00:18:14.240
have table which is
multi-million rows table

00:18:14.240 --> 00:18:17.480
in this example, in this demo.

00:18:17.480 --> 00:18:20.520
And we'll have many business
users and analysts which

00:18:20.520 --> 00:18:22.380
need to work with this data.

00:18:22.380 --> 00:18:24.980
So let's say they all
have complex dashboards.

00:18:24.980 --> 00:18:27.740
And dashboards have many,
many visual elements.

00:18:27.740 --> 00:18:34.250
So we simulated single dashboard
sends 10 different queries, OK?

00:18:34.250 --> 00:18:35.820
And also said, you
know, dashboards

00:18:35.820 --> 00:18:37.630
are not static reports.

00:18:37.630 --> 00:18:38.690
People work with them.

00:18:38.690 --> 00:18:42.740
So we said, you know, what
if each one of those users

00:18:42.740 --> 00:18:47.980
changes dashboard
once every second.

00:18:47.980 --> 00:18:51.630
And we say, you know
this is very unrealistic,

00:18:51.630 --> 00:18:53.380
nobody will change
it every second.

00:18:53.380 --> 00:18:55.350
But we'll say, let's push it.

00:18:55.350 --> 00:19:00.260
OK, so many users with
dashboards, each dashboard

00:19:00.260 --> 00:19:03.820
sends 10 queries every second.

00:19:03.820 --> 00:19:07.960
So what we're looking
here is Stackdriver,

00:19:07.960 --> 00:19:10.700
which captures few metrics.

00:19:10.700 --> 00:19:13.110
First metric we are
going to focus on

00:19:13.110 --> 00:19:18.750
is this blue line, which shows
the throughput measured in QPS.

00:19:18.750 --> 00:19:23.550
And we can see that we're
starting with 100 QPS, which

00:19:23.550 --> 00:19:26.360
roughly represents
10 users sends

00:19:26.360 --> 00:19:29.480
10 queries each every second.

00:19:29.480 --> 00:19:31.910
All those queries are
completely randomly generated.

00:19:31.910 --> 00:19:33.410
They're not the same.

00:19:33.410 --> 00:19:37.100
You know there is nobody
using same query result.

00:19:37.100 --> 00:19:40.520
It's totally BI Engine
working full speed.

00:19:40.520 --> 00:19:42.190
100 queries a second.

00:19:42.190 --> 00:19:44.020
Actually 100 is too small.

00:19:44.020 --> 00:19:45.880
Let's increase it to 1,000.

00:19:45.880 --> 00:19:46.570
OK?

00:19:46.570 --> 00:19:48.500
OK, you see, we're
working with 1,000.

00:19:48.500 --> 00:19:53.170
Now it's like 100 users
working with those dashboards.

00:19:53.170 --> 00:19:54.460
Let's ramp it up.

00:19:54.460 --> 00:19:55.580
You know, how high?

00:19:55.580 --> 00:19:57.245
OK 2,000.

00:19:57.245 --> 00:20:00.350
200 users every
second, 10 queries.

00:20:00.350 --> 00:20:01.800
System is working.

00:20:01.800 --> 00:20:04.830
But now how well is it working?

00:20:04.830 --> 00:20:07.070
Maybe it's falling apart.

00:20:07.070 --> 00:20:09.360
So to see how those
systems scale,

00:20:09.360 --> 00:20:12.680
we would need to look into
different metric, which

00:20:12.680 --> 00:20:15.050
is query response metrics.

00:20:15.050 --> 00:20:17.270
And you know you see
those three lines

00:20:17.270 --> 00:20:18.770
at the bottom of the chart?

00:20:18.770 --> 00:20:23.780
It's kind of hard to see because
they're kind of very low, so

00:20:23.780 --> 00:20:27.020
let's zoom in onto them, OK?

00:20:27.020 --> 00:20:31.700
So those three lines
represent the percentiles

00:20:31.700 --> 00:20:33.020
of query response.

00:20:33.020 --> 00:20:35.510
The lowest one, which
I think is orange,

00:20:35.510 --> 00:20:38.900
shows the 50th percentile,
which is medium.

00:20:38.900 --> 00:20:39.770
OK?

00:20:39.770 --> 00:20:41.270
And then the next one is red.

00:20:41.270 --> 00:20:43.760
It shows 95th percentile,
because it's also

00:20:43.760 --> 00:20:47.180
very important to look at
latency in the large scale

00:20:47.180 --> 00:20:48.140
systems.

00:20:48.140 --> 00:20:53.550
And finally the blue, the violet
one is the 99th percentile.

00:20:53.550 --> 00:20:56.930
So first thing that we
observe is no matter

00:20:56.930 --> 00:21:00.350
how far we ramp
up our query load,

00:21:00.350 --> 00:21:03.440
they stand largely
horizontal, largely the same.

00:21:03.440 --> 00:21:08.180
Might be at 2,000 QPS they
increase, but only a tiny bit.

00:21:08.180 --> 00:21:10.460
Also absolute times
are really good.

00:21:10.460 --> 00:21:14.140
Like 50th percentile is
only 35 milliseconds.

00:21:14.140 --> 00:21:18.590
95th percentile is lower
than 100 milliseconds.

00:21:18.590 --> 00:21:22.250
And 99th percentile
is 150 milliseconds.

00:21:22.250 --> 00:21:26.180
Remember this is many,
many million rows table

00:21:26.180 --> 00:21:30.270
and we're running at 2,000 QPS.

00:21:30.270 --> 00:21:32.220
2,000 queries every second.

00:21:32.220 --> 00:21:36.210
So I can see that BI
Engine holds really well.

00:21:36.210 --> 00:21:40.080
So that was throughput demo,
and I'm passing it over to Vinay

00:21:40.080 --> 00:21:41.653
to show two other demos.

00:21:41.653 --> 00:21:43.320
VINAY BALASUBRAMANIAM:
Thank you, Mosha.

00:21:43.320 --> 00:21:47.580
And you saw how BI Engine is
able to scale much more higher

00:21:47.580 --> 00:21:49.360
to provide higher concurrency.

00:21:49.360 --> 00:21:52.110
Now I want to introduce
Madeleine Corneli, who's

00:21:52.110 --> 00:21:54.270
a product manager at
Tableau, to showcase

00:21:54.270 --> 00:21:57.290
an initial preview of how
Tableau and BigQuery BI

00:21:57.290 --> 00:21:58.207
Engine works together.

00:21:58.207 --> 00:21:59.998
MADELEINE CORNELI:
We're going to start off

00:21:59.998 --> 00:22:01.332
in Tableau Desktop.

00:22:01.332 --> 00:22:02.790
And the first thing
I'm going to do

00:22:02.790 --> 00:22:05.445
is I'm going to connect to
my Google BigQuery instance.

00:22:11.270 --> 00:22:14.390
Then I can choose what data
I want to analyze today.

00:22:14.390 --> 00:22:16.610
To leverage BI Engine,
all I need to do

00:22:16.610 --> 00:22:18.910
is ensure I connect
to the billing project

00:22:18.910 --> 00:22:21.230
where BI Engine is enabled.

00:22:21.230 --> 00:22:24.500
Then I can select the
project and dataset

00:22:24.500 --> 00:22:27.060
where my data lives.

00:22:27.060 --> 00:22:29.120
I'll then pull out
the table or tables

00:22:29.120 --> 00:22:32.870
that I want to analyze today.

00:22:32.870 --> 00:22:36.350
Hopping into our first
sheet, I can pull out

00:22:36.350 --> 00:22:38.780
the number of records
field to quickly see

00:22:38.780 --> 00:22:41.180
that we're working with
a dataset of over 87

00:22:41.180 --> 00:22:42.650
million rows.

00:22:42.650 --> 00:22:46.140
That's a pretty hefty
dataset to work with.

00:22:46.140 --> 00:22:50.550
Now this data contains
lots of customer reviews

00:22:50.550 --> 00:22:52.560
of various health and
beauty products sold

00:22:52.560 --> 00:22:53.950
by a company called YLM.

00:22:56.580 --> 00:23:00.000
I can use Tableau to quickly
aggregate the overall reviews

00:23:00.000 --> 00:23:01.245
for all of these products.

00:23:04.460 --> 00:23:07.610
Looking at the average review,
I can see what of our products

00:23:07.610 --> 00:23:14.520
have scored very high and which
ones haven't scored as high.

00:23:14.520 --> 00:23:16.720
Tableau also makes it
easy to quickly switch

00:23:16.720 --> 00:23:21.017
between various
visualization types.

00:23:21.017 --> 00:23:22.350
Let's call this Product Reviews.

00:23:27.210 --> 00:23:28.730
Now, each of these
products lives

00:23:28.730 --> 00:23:32.600
in various different categories.

00:23:32.600 --> 00:23:34.850
Tableau makes it very
easy for me to group

00:23:34.850 --> 00:23:37.640
these various categories
together into something

00:23:37.640 --> 00:23:39.203
we call a hierarchy.

00:23:42.210 --> 00:23:44.335
I'm going to create a new
hierarchy called Product.

00:23:47.760 --> 00:23:49.380
And by doing so,
it makes it easy

00:23:49.380 --> 00:23:51.810
for me to drill up and
down between various levels

00:23:51.810 --> 00:23:56.023
of granularity in my data.

00:23:56.023 --> 00:23:57.440
Now for this view,
I'd like to get

00:23:57.440 --> 00:23:59.840
a sense of the overall
quantity of sales I have

00:23:59.840 --> 00:24:01.310
for each one of my categories.

00:24:03.850 --> 00:24:07.300
I'd also like to add the context
of the average rating, as well.

00:24:11.290 --> 00:24:13.590
Now every time I use
aggregate rating here,

00:24:13.590 --> 00:24:17.010
I'm typically going to want to
look at the average overall.

00:24:17.010 --> 00:24:22.820
So I'm going to set the default
property to use average.

00:24:22.820 --> 00:24:27.293
I also want to change the
color scheme, as well.

00:24:27.293 --> 00:24:29.960
I want to make sure that I don't
miss any of these products that

00:24:29.960 --> 00:24:33.200
may have lower reviews as
I go through my analysis.

00:24:36.630 --> 00:24:39.090
Now I can see that there are
certain product categories

00:24:39.090 --> 00:24:41.820
where I have a high
quantity of sales

00:24:41.820 --> 00:24:44.700
but the aggregate
rating isn't very high.

00:24:44.700 --> 00:24:48.240
And that might be something
I want to investigate.

00:24:48.240 --> 00:24:52.395
Let's call this Product
Reviews and Quantity.

00:24:56.180 --> 00:24:59.190
I'd also like to look at
this data geographically.

00:24:59.190 --> 00:25:02.000
This dataset already
includes state information,

00:25:02.000 --> 00:25:05.440
so I can simply drag
state out onto the view,

00:25:05.440 --> 00:25:09.050
and Tableau will
plot that for me.

00:25:09.050 --> 00:25:11.160
Now I can also add
quantity, as well.

00:25:11.160 --> 00:25:16.635
So we can see which states
have higher quantities sold.

00:25:16.635 --> 00:25:21.005
I'm going to add aggregate
rating to the view again.

00:25:21.005 --> 00:25:22.630
And I can see that
generally the states

00:25:22.630 --> 00:25:25.360
where I have higher
numbers of sales,

00:25:25.360 --> 00:25:28.720
I also typically have
better product ratings.

00:25:28.720 --> 00:25:30.400
However there are
some problem areas

00:25:30.400 --> 00:25:32.350
that I may want to investigate.

00:25:32.350 --> 00:25:36.910
I can easily drill down
to a lower level of detail

00:25:36.910 --> 00:25:39.910
to figure out if there are
specific cities where I have

00:25:39.910 --> 00:25:42.923
high quantity but low ratings.

00:25:42.923 --> 00:25:45.340
Let's bump up the size a little
bit so it's easier to see.

00:25:54.300 --> 00:25:58.760
We'll call this Product
Ratings and Quantity by City.

00:25:58.760 --> 00:26:01.660
Finally, I want to pull together
all of these visualizations

00:26:01.660 --> 00:26:04.990
onto something we call
a Tableau dashboard.

00:26:04.990 --> 00:26:07.210
Using a dashboard makes
it easy to compare

00:26:07.210 --> 00:26:09.280
multiple visualizations
at the same time.

00:26:12.760 --> 00:26:15.810
It also gives me the
ability to add interactivity

00:26:15.810 --> 00:26:19.750
between these
various worksheets.

00:26:19.750 --> 00:26:22.720
Doing so effectively creates
an interactive application

00:26:22.720 --> 00:26:26.600
that lets me visually
explore my data.

00:26:26.600 --> 00:26:30.870
I can look at some of
our problem subcategories

00:26:30.870 --> 00:26:36.160
and select a particular region
to explore in more detail.

00:26:36.160 --> 00:26:39.628
And finally, I can
pinpoint exact products

00:26:39.628 --> 00:26:40.545
that have low reviews.

00:26:43.320 --> 00:26:45.550
And so with that, in
just a few minutes,

00:26:45.550 --> 00:26:47.400
we've built an
interactive dashboard

00:26:47.400 --> 00:26:52.770
on top of over 87 million rows
of data thanks to BI Engine.

00:26:52.770 --> 00:26:55.020
Now I can publish this to
Tableau Server or Tableau

00:26:55.020 --> 00:26:56.640
Online and share it
with other people

00:26:56.640 --> 00:26:58.333
throughout my organization.

00:26:58.333 --> 00:27:00.000
VINAY BALASUBRAMANIAM:
Thanks Madeleine.

00:27:00.000 --> 00:27:01.968
That was a great demo
of how Tableau and BI

00:27:01.968 --> 00:27:03.010
Engine can work together.

00:27:03.010 --> 00:27:05.100
Now, I want to introduce
Kevin Marr who's

00:27:05.100 --> 00:27:09.600
a product manager in Looker to
talk about how Looker and BI

00:27:09.600 --> 00:27:11.735
Engine work together.

00:27:11.735 --> 00:27:12.360
KEVIN MARR: Hi.

00:27:12.360 --> 00:27:14.010
I'm Kevin, a product
manager at Looker,

00:27:14.010 --> 00:27:17.250
and I'll be showing you Looker's
integration with BigQuery's BI

00:27:17.250 --> 00:27:18.570
Engine.

00:27:18.570 --> 00:27:21.750
First, Looker is a modern
data analytics platform

00:27:21.750 --> 00:27:23.985
that operates in database.

00:27:23.985 --> 00:27:27.440
This means that Looker does
not ingest your source data.

00:27:27.440 --> 00:27:30.200
If you load a report or
visualization in Looker,

00:27:30.200 --> 00:27:32.460
a SQL query is
being sent directly

00:27:32.460 --> 00:27:34.312
to your data warehouse.

00:27:34.312 --> 00:27:36.270
And as you can imagine,
having a very fast data

00:27:36.270 --> 00:27:38.400
warehouse means that
you'll get a very

00:27:38.400 --> 00:27:41.230
good experience in Looker.

00:27:41.230 --> 00:27:43.490
So I'm in Looker's
Explore page, which

00:27:43.490 --> 00:27:45.210
allows business
users to self-serve

00:27:45.210 --> 00:27:48.050
by exploring their
data, building

00:27:48.050 --> 00:27:50.870
their own visualizations,
and ultimately answering

00:27:50.870 --> 00:27:52.630
their own questions.

00:27:52.630 --> 00:27:55.750
The Explore page is powered
by Looker's modeling layer

00:27:55.750 --> 00:27:58.840
called LookML, which
provides an abstraction

00:27:58.840 --> 00:28:02.950
layer between your database
schema and the business terms

00:28:02.950 --> 00:28:06.175
that your users
are familiar with.

00:28:06.175 --> 00:28:07.550
All I have to do
to build a query

00:28:07.550 --> 00:28:11.545
here is select dimensions
and measures and add filters.

00:28:11.545 --> 00:28:14.330
Say I want to know the
total revenue we've ever

00:28:14.330 --> 00:28:18.140
made in our fictitious
e-commerce store.

00:28:18.140 --> 00:28:23.595
I can just click on the Total
Sales measure and hit Run.

00:28:23.595 --> 00:28:25.220
Behind the scenes,
Looker has generated

00:28:25.220 --> 00:28:30.250
a query, a SQL query for me,
and has sent that to BigQuery,

00:28:30.250 --> 00:28:33.460
in this case, BI Engine,
and has given me the answer,

00:28:33.460 --> 00:28:37.441
in this case, $9.6 million.

00:28:37.441 --> 00:28:39.320
It's easy for me to add
things like filters.

00:28:39.320 --> 00:28:42.710
Say that I want to look at
this over some constrained

00:28:42.710 --> 00:28:43.970
timeframe.

00:28:43.970 --> 00:28:47.150
I can click on a filter.

00:28:47.150 --> 00:28:50.790
I can pick any of these easy
point-and-click options.

00:28:50.790 --> 00:28:55.130
I'll just pick, say, April 2020.

00:28:55.130 --> 00:29:03.130
So starting on the 1st of April
and ending on the 1st of May.

00:29:03.130 --> 00:29:06.010
As I've done those interactions
with the user interface,

00:29:06.010 --> 00:29:10.280
again, Looker is adapting the
SQL to reflect what I want.

00:29:10.280 --> 00:29:14.230
So when I hit Run, that SQL gets
sent to BigQuery's BI Engine

00:29:14.230 --> 00:29:16.740
and the result is posted here.

00:29:16.740 --> 00:29:17.280
Great.

00:29:17.280 --> 00:29:24.470
So in April 2020 my
business made $441,000.

00:29:24.470 --> 00:29:28.600
Also if I jump over to
the BigQuery console,

00:29:28.600 --> 00:29:31.200
we can take a look
at those queries,

00:29:31.200 --> 00:29:34.520
and you just get a sense for how
fast these queries really are.

00:29:34.520 --> 00:29:37.030
I click on this, you can see
that that query I just ran

00:29:37.030 --> 00:29:39.253
took only a quarter of a second.

00:29:41.992 --> 00:29:43.700
Now let's take a step
further and maybe I

00:29:43.700 --> 00:29:45.560
want to create a visualization.

00:29:45.560 --> 00:29:47.410
Maybe I want to
create a time series.

00:29:47.410 --> 00:29:50.420
I can do that just by adding
the date dimension to my query.

00:29:50.420 --> 00:29:53.670
Again, hitting Run.

00:29:53.670 --> 00:29:56.456
Looker adapted the SQL.

00:29:56.456 --> 00:29:59.933
And the result is displayed
here in Looker's user interface.

00:29:59.933 --> 00:30:01.350
I want to visualize
it, I can just

00:30:01.350 --> 00:30:05.650
open up the Visualization
section, where

00:30:05.650 --> 00:30:08.830
we have a myriad of viz types.

00:30:08.830 --> 00:30:11.170
In this case Looker
things that a line chart

00:30:11.170 --> 00:30:14.410
is the best [? base of ?]
my data, which I like.

00:30:14.410 --> 00:30:16.581
And I'll go ahead and
save this to a dashboard.

00:30:19.230 --> 00:30:25.130
I have a nice little
test dashboard I added.

00:30:25.130 --> 00:30:26.600
I'll save it there.

00:30:26.600 --> 00:30:29.745
And I'll pop that
open in my other tab.

00:30:29.745 --> 00:30:31.620
I'll go ahead and refresh
this dashboard just

00:30:31.620 --> 00:30:32.880
to rerun these queries again.

00:30:32.880 --> 00:30:34.860
We've turned off all
caching on this demo,

00:30:34.860 --> 00:30:38.780
just to show off the
query speed in BI Engine.

00:30:38.780 --> 00:30:41.150
The dashboard's done
rendering already.

00:30:41.150 --> 00:30:43.780
And at the bottom I
can see my new tile.

00:30:43.780 --> 00:30:45.870
I actually added two from
a previous recording,

00:30:45.870 --> 00:30:48.967
but pretend it's just one.

00:30:48.967 --> 00:30:50.550
Dashboards in Looker
are also designed

00:30:50.550 --> 00:30:52.620
to be heavily interactive.

00:30:52.620 --> 00:30:55.110
So say that right
now we're looking

00:30:55.110 --> 00:31:00.370
at just the data over Facebook,
I can deselect that selection

00:31:00.370 --> 00:31:03.990
and that'll cross-filter
across all my other queries.

00:31:03.990 --> 00:31:06.090
And I can see the data
broadly across all

00:31:06.090 --> 00:31:10.060
the demographic profiles.

00:31:10.060 --> 00:31:12.550
I can also adjust filters
directly in the top.

00:31:12.550 --> 00:31:16.810
Say I want to look at this
specifically in some city, say,

00:31:16.810 --> 00:31:20.020
San Francisco, where I live.

00:31:20.020 --> 00:31:21.870
I can start typing
that in, and Looker

00:31:21.870 --> 00:31:25.010
will start offering suggestions
based on my underlying data.

00:31:31.330 --> 00:31:35.220
So I can click that,
rerun the dashboard,

00:31:35.220 --> 00:31:39.780
and see how this is trending
just in San Francisco.

00:31:39.780 --> 00:31:40.410
Great.

00:31:40.410 --> 00:31:41.110
That's it.

00:31:41.110 --> 00:31:42.348
Thank you very much.

00:31:42.348 --> 00:31:43.890
VINAY BALASUBRAMANIAM:
Thanks, Kevin.

00:31:43.890 --> 00:31:47.860
So in conclusion, BI
Engine is available today.

00:31:47.860 --> 00:31:49.560
You can try it today
with Data Studio.

00:31:49.560 --> 00:31:50.960
It's a GA product.

00:31:50.960 --> 00:31:52.180
It's available today.

00:31:52.180 --> 00:31:53.730
But what we're really
showcasing here

00:31:53.730 --> 00:31:59.060
is how BI Engine SQL API
makes it possible to work,

00:31:59.060 --> 00:32:02.340
to give the same acceleration,
same benefits with other BI

00:32:02.340 --> 00:32:04.403
tools, including
Looker, Tableau,

00:32:04.403 --> 00:32:06.570
and other BI tools who work
with the query directly.

00:32:06.570 --> 00:32:08.010
So that's coming.

00:32:08.010 --> 00:32:11.208
Sometime end of this year
we'll have a initial preview.

00:32:11.208 --> 00:32:12.750
And also there are
additional session

00:32:12.750 --> 00:32:15.370
to learn more about, if you
want to know about how BigQuery

00:32:15.370 --> 00:32:20.430
is evolving in other areas,
there's a session DA 200,

00:32:20.430 --> 00:32:22.720
which talks about
BigQuery's roadmap.

00:32:22.720 --> 00:32:25.500
And if you want to learn
about how Looker is evolving

00:32:25.500 --> 00:32:28.650
and their roadmap,
there's a session, DA 239.

00:32:28.650 --> 00:32:32.330
So do watch out, and
again, thanks for joining.

